/* book exam traps: Inverse relation: Higher Q means smaller BW, not larger. If BW falls by a factor of 2, Q rises by a factor of 2 at the same f₀. | Unit mix-up: BW = f₀/Q gives hertz only when f₀ is in hertz. ωBW = ω₀/Q gives rad/s. Multiplying or dividing by 2π at the wrong step changes the answer. | Series and parallel R: Qseries = ω₀L/R falls as R rises, while Qparallel = R/(ω₀L) rises as R rises. Using the wrong formula reverses the effect of R. | Approximation limit: f₁,₂ = f₀ ± BW/2 is only good for Q ≥ 10. For low Q the exact half-power frequencies are not evenly spaced around f₀. | Selectivity confusion: f₀/BW = Q and BW/f₀ = 1/Q are both quoted. A fractional bandwidth of 0.02 is 2 %, not 0.02 %. State which one you use. */
